WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.161
Over and under.

2
00:00:01.321 --> 00:00:04.154
Weaving is going over and under, again and again.

3
00:00:04.314 --> 00:00:06.543
The threads hold each other in place.

4
00:00:06.703 --> 00:00:11.161
That is how loose threads become something strong, like cloth or a basket.

5
00:00:11.861 --> 00:00:13.208
Warp and weft.

6
00:00:13.368 --> 00:00:15.736
On a loom, some threads are stretched tight.

7
00:00:15.896 --> 00:00:17.197
They are the warp.

8
00:00:17.357 --> 00:00:19.632
The thread you weave across is the weft.

9
00:00:19.792 --> 00:00:22.486
It goes over one warp thread and under the next.

10
00:00:22.646 --> 00:00:24.364
The next row goes the other way.

11
00:00:25.064 --> 00:00:26.457
Woven everywhere.

12
00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:28.428
People weave all over the world.

13
00:00:28.588 --> 00:00:29.796
Baskets from grass.

14
00:00:29.956 --> 00:00:31.349
Scarves from wool.

15
00:00:31.509 --> 00:00:35.642
In Ghana, long strips of cloth are woven on a loom and sewn together.

16
00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:37.660
Some birds even weave their nests.

17
00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:39.660
When it goes wrong.

18
00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.817
If a strip falls out, ask why.

19
00:00:41.977 --> 00:00:44.624
Did it go over and under, or just lie on top?

20
00:00:44.784 --> 00:00:47.153
If there are gaps, push the strips close.

21
00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:49.867
Find the one thing that went wrong, and fix it.
